Before I clutter up Sunsite's Incomming with this, I'm releasing it to 
the net only as a message.  Version 0.2 and subsequent versions will be 
u'led to Sunsite.

ISDN-Product Howto
Version: 0.1
Date: 12 November 1995
Writen by:  David Flood  davidf@ssc.com


Note:  All copyrights and trademarks used in this document are the property
of the respective owners.

This document is intended as a referance to SOME of the products availible
to connect computers running Linux to a ISDN circut.  All opinions are my
own unless attributed to someone else.  Additional documentation, success
and / or failure stories, and suggested additions can be sent to me.  The
order in which products are listed in this document has no meaning outside
of the fact that I went through my files and took each product as I came
to it.

Product:  3Com Impact External ISDN Modem

Model Number:  3c871, 3c872

Manufacturer:  3Com

Interface Type:  RS-232-C Serial

Linux Driver Availible:  N/A

Source(s) of Information:  3Com documentation, actual use of a 3c871

Specifications:
		Internal BRI NT1
		Configuration uses a superset of the AT command set
		Support for Multilink PPP and V.120 protocols
		Dynamic Bandwidth Allocation of channels
		Internal 14.4 V.32bis analog fax modem (3c872 only)
		Analog voice output port

Comments:  
	I have used the 3c871 model but not the 3c872.  Once the modem is
connected to the computer, any communications program can be used to configure
the modem using an extended set of the ATSx= commands.  These registers are
well documented in the manual.  In addition, the modem comes with MSDOS and
MS Windows install programs that basicly give the user a graphical interface
and issue the AT commands behind the scenes.  There are two drawbacks to this
product.  (1) Since it is external, the type of UART in your serial port will
determine the top speed for the data transfer.  (2) The product does not
support any form of Bonding.

Product:  3Com Impact internal ISDN Modem

Model Number:  3c861 (S/T interface)
	       3c862 (U interface)

Manufacturer:  3Com

Interface Type:  Emulates network card (?)

Linux Driver Availible:  NO

Source(s) of Information:  3Com web page

Specifications:  Not availible

Comments:  
	I have one of the 3c862 (U interface) cards on order.  Once I have
recived it, more information will be added.

Product:  Courier I-Modem with ISDN/V.34

Model Number:	U-Interfaces
	       00698-0 (External with analog device port)
	       00699-0 (External without analog device port)
	       00700-0 (Internal with analog device port)
	       00701-0 (Internal without analog device port)
		S/T Interface
	       001102-0 (External)
	       001101-0 (Internal)

Manufacturer:  USR

Interface Type:  Unknown

Linux Driver Availible:  Unknown

Source(s) of Information:  USR Homepage

Specifications:  Integrated NT-1 (U-Interface models only)
		 BRI line support
		 V.110 and V.120 standards
		 
Comments:  I've tried to contact USR to obtain a evaluation model and have had
no luck.

Product:  Everyware 1000 Internal

Model Number:  1030 (S/T Interface)
	       1040 (U Interface)
	       1050 (S/T Interface with compression)
	       1060 (U Interface with compression)

Manufacturer:  Combinet

Interface Type:  Communications port for configuration
		 Network port for data

Linux Driver Availible:  Yes

Source(s) of Information:  Combinet documentation, driver documentation,
			   actual use of a 1060

Specifications:  U Interface models have integrated NT-1
		 Passive PPP mode

Comments:  This card, with the 3.01 firmware upgrade, could be a good choice
for an ISP but not a user unless both ends have Combinet products.  
However, since the 1060 requires that the 'PPP init' signal come from the 
other device, you can't hook two 1060's up and have them talk.

Product:  DataFire Internal ISDN LAN Adapter

Model Number:  N/A

Manufacturer:  Digi International

Interface Type:  Unknown

Linux Driver Availible:  NO

Source(s) of Information:  Digi documentation and phone calls to Digi Support

Specifications:  U-Interface with integrated NT-1

Comments:  This card is aimed at connecting a single computer to a MS or Novell
based system.  The card has no security features.  It relies on the called
computer to supply them.  The support personnel that I talked to were very
hostile towards using this card under Linux.

Product:  Pipeline external

Model Number:  Pipeline 25 ISDN

Manufacturer:  Ascend

Interface Type:  Communications port for config
		 Ethernet port for data

Linux Driver Availible:  N/A

Source(s) of Information:  Ascend liturature

Specifications:  S/T or U interface
		 10 Base-T or AUI network interface
		 DB-9 RS-232-C configuration port
		 IP, IPX, and network bridging
		 Additional protocols and security features at additional cost

Comments:  Except for the fact that this is an external unit that requires two
separate interfaces, it should be usable.


