-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

[ Moderator's note: I already posted this once, but to the wrong newsgroup. :-(
  My apologies.  --liw ]

As the maintainer of BRLTTY, a program which gives blind Linux users 
access to the console via a soft Braille display, I have been approached 
by a small company based in England who are involved in the production of 
X-terminals for banks etc.  They have adapted their terminals, which run 
on Linux-based PCs, to include magnification, zoom etc. to help 
partially-sighted users, and are exploring the possibilities of adding 
Braille and synthetic speech support.

Such a development involves some far from inconsiderable problems from the
point of view of the developer: while magnification is obviously graphics
based, Braille or speech support means storing information about the text
displayed, rather than just the pixel-patterns, which are of little use. 
The first question, then, must be `is there a market?'

Since these X-terminals run on Linux, they have already been `ported' to 
Linux, so do allow use of Xfree86 on such a system.  The implications of 
this would be that blind users, for the first time, would have direct 
access to a system running X.

While, of course, this would be a commercial venture, the company
concerned are also considering releasing some of their work as free
software.  Either way, though, they need to know that there is sufficient
interest to make the project viable - and that's where this announcement
fits in.  If you're interested, don't just keep quiet and hope something
will happen - tell them!  If you send email to me (nn201@cus.cam.ac.uk),
I'll make sure they receive your comments.  Hopefully, we'll be able to
convince them that this is worth doing. 

To a bright future for equal access and opportunities!,

Nikhil.

