Global Technologies Ltd., Inc. is offering ksh93 for the Linux
community in either a.out or elf format (customer's preference).
This is a binary only product.  The current release is ksh 93
release D.  The introductory cost is $30 plus $5 shipping and handling.  

We are also offering a variant of ksh called tksh93.  The tksh93 
is a ksh93 based implementation that can interpret and execute tcl/tk 
scripts.  This is the first commercial ksh that we are aware of to provide 
this capability.  The tksh is available on Linux, UnixWare, and
Solaris for $50 plus $5 shipping and handling.  

In addition, Global Technologies Ltd., Inc. will be a facilitator
for base objects as part of the standard ksh93.  [ksh objects are
briefly described in  "The New KornShell," Bolsky and Korn 1995, Prentice
Hall].  An object, in this sense, can be thought of as a base class variable.  
Other variables may be defined using this base class.  

As an example, consider the file_t base class object.  The definition
of this class is:
	file_t.name	
	file_t.atime
	file_t.ctime
	file_t.mtime
	file_t.image

To define a variable of this type, you would simply use:
	typeset -C file_t file

By assigning a value to "name" member, you will be able to
access additional information.  For example,
	file.name=$HOME/.profile
	print ${file.atime} 	# print the access time of the file
	print ${file.ctime} 	# print the change time of file
	print ${file.mtime} 	# print the modification time of file
	print ${file.image}	# this will print the current contents
			# of the file.

Additional base class type can be added by the Linux users.  We
will be a focal point for the collection and redistribution of
objects within the Linux community.

In January, we will be offering a series of additional software
tools, including:
